  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 AM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nEarly morning water vapor and RAP analysis feature midlevel low \npressure centered over northern Quebec, sending troughing southward \nthrough the Lower Great Lakes. A compact shortwave is also noted off \nof British Columbia, while Isaias spins over the Gulf. Closer to \nhome, ridging is building over the central CONUS, supporting surface \nhigh pressure over the Great Lakes. Nighttime microphysics shows \nmostly clear skies behind an exiting patch of midlevel lake-enhanced \nclouds and showers. Temperatures are already falling into the lower \n40s over much of the interior-UP and into the upper 40s lakeside, \nthough some spots are already well into the 30s. Expect temperatures \nto bottom out in the mid/upper 30s over most of the interior-UP, and \nthe lower 40s lakeside, while some spots flirt with the freezing \npoint in the interior-west. \n\nAs we head into the daytime hours, the ridge begins to amplify and \nshift eastward while the aforementioned British Columbia shortwave \nmoves onshore and deepens. Winds shift more southerly in turn, which \nshould help temperatures climb a couple degrees higher than in \nprevious days; expect highs in the mid 60s for most, apart from \nnearer Lake Michigan and over the Superior shoreline, where \ntemperatures may struggle to get out of the upper 50s. Model \nsoundings support a sharp dewpoint drop into the afternoon hours, \nwith RH in turn falling to near and even below 30% in the interior-\nwest. Light winds limit fire weather concerns, but another dry day \ndoes not bode well as we head into the weekend. Overnight into early \nSaturday, temperatures come in much milder than tonight, ranging in \nthe 40s for most as a warmer airmass continues to work in.\n\nSaturday, the surface high pressure area over the NE CONUS continues \nto build and shift eastward while a Clipper Low splits over the \nnorthern Mountain West and moves into the Northern Plains. This \nsetup will result in a tightly packed surface pressure gradient over \nthe northern Midwest and strong WAA into the Great Lakes region. \nCombined with mostly clear skies, these southerly winds are likely \n(60-80% chance) to cause highs exceeding 80 degrees in the interior \nwest over the weekend. The eastern U.P. will see highs limited to \nthe mid-70s as the southerly flow will be moderated by Lake Michigan \nsurface temperatures in the 50s and 60s. Elevated to critical fire \nweather conditions are possible over the weekend, particularity in \nthe interior west. RHs will fall below 40% in the interior west both \nSaturday and Sunday afternoons, with some areas possibly reaching \nbelow 35%. Overnight RH is expected to remain at or below 70% \novernight for the western U.P. Gusts up to 30mph are possible for \nthe interior west Saturday, and could be locally higher in areas \nprone to downsloping. Wind speeds will be somewhat lower for Sunday, \nbut gusts could still reach 25mph. Fire weather partners and the \npublic should continue to monitor the forecast for fire weather \nconcerns this weekend due to potentially elevated to critical fire \nweather and ongoing drought conditions. \n\nOur next best chance of precipitation is predicted to arrive late \nMonday into Tuesday as a shortwave trough associated with an eastern \nCanadian low moves across the area along with a cold front. \nNortherly flow behind the front could also result in lake effect \nprecipitation chances through the early workweek as seasonably cool \ntemperatures return.",
